    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Executive Summary & Verdict\n\nFor a standard **Google L6 PM (Staff / Group PM)**, this is an **above-average to strong offer**, particularly on equity. \n\nHowever, because you are currently a **Meta IC6 PM (Principal PM)**, happy where you are, and motivated heavily by compensation, **you should not accept this offer as is.** \n\n* **The Leveling Mismatch:** Meta IC6 generally maps closer to **Google L7 (Senior Staff / Group PM)** than L6. Google L6 often corresponds to Meta IC5 (or a high-IC5 / low-IC6 borderline).\n* **The Bottom Line:** You hold significant leverage: you were recruited inbound, you enjoy your current job, and you have strong compensation at Meta. You should **negotiate aggressively** or **walk away / push for L7 calibration** if they cannot meet your targets.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Numbers: Understanding Google\u2019s Vesting Model\n\nGoogle\u2019s offer letter annualized number ($552,000/year on average) obscures how Google actually pays equity. Google typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (commonly 38% / 32% / 20% / 10% or 33% / 33% / 22% / 12%).\n\nAssuming the standard **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** schedule:\n\n| Year | Base Salary | Target Bonus (20%) | Stock Vesting (%) | Stock Value | **Total Compensation (TC)** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $235,000 | $47,000 | 38% | $410,400 | **~$692,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $235,000 | $47,000 | 32% | $345,600 | **~$627,600** |\n| **Year 3** | $235,000 | $47,000 | 20% | $216,000 | **~$498,000** *(+ Refreshes)* |\n| **Year 4** | $235,000 | $47,000 | 10% | $108,000 | **~$390,000** *(+ Refreshes)* |\n\n#### The Refresher Reality\n* Google refresh grants vest over several years and are meant to soften the Year 3 and Year 4 drop-off. \n* Meta\u2019s equity, by contrast, vests evenly at **25% per year** with quarterly vesting, and Meta\u2019s refresher culture for IC6 is among the most aggressive in Big Tech. \n* At Google, relying on refreshes to prevent a compensation drop in Year 3/4 introduces performance and macroeconomic risk.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Google L6 vs. Meta IC6 Benchmarks\n\n* **Google L6 PM Market Data (Bay Area):**\n  * **Base:** $210,000 \u2013 $250,000\n  * **Bonus:** 20%\n  * **Equity (Annualized):** $180,000 \u2013 $260,000/yr\n  * **Median Total Comp:** ~$510,000 \u2013 $540,000/yr\n  * *Your offer ($552k annualized average) sits around the 70th\u201375th percentile for L6.*\n* **Meta IC6 PM Benchmarks:**\n  * Meta IC6 total comp frequently spans **$580,000 to $800,000+** per year (combining higher base, strong performance multipliers, and larger recurring equity grants).\n* **Google L7 PM Benchmarks:**\n  * Median L7 total comp sits between **$720,000 and $850,000+**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Is Missing from This Offer?\n\n1. **Sign-On Bonus:** It is standard for Google to offer a **$50,000 \u2013 $100,000+** signing bonus for L6 candidates coming from peer Tier-1 companies (especially Meta), specifically to offset unvested stock and bridge compensation.\n2. **Base Salary Headroom:** Your base ($235k) sits near the midpoint of the posted $183k\u2013$271k band.\n3. **Level Alignment:** An IC6 candidate entering as L6 represents a down-level in career scope.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Strategy: How to Negotiate\n\nSince you are content at Meta, you can negotiate from an anchor of strength.\n\n#### Step 1: Inquire About Leveling (L6 vs. L7)\nPolitely raise the scope difference between Meta IC6 (Principal) and Google L6 (Staff):\n> *\"Given my current scope as an IC6 at Meta, my expectation coming into the process was calibration at L7. Was L7 considered by the hiring committee, and what would it take to bridge that gap?\"*\n\n#### Step 2: Make a Concrete Counter-Proposal for L6\nIf Google is set on L6, require top-of-band compensation and a sign-on bonus to make the switch compelling:\n\n* **Base Salary:** Ask for **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** (closer to the $271k maximum).\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** Ask for **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000** (to offset leaving your unvested Meta RSUs).\n* **Equity:** Ask for **$1,250,000 over 4 years** (bringing annualized equity to ~$312k/yr).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Sample Negotiation Script\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the product challenges in this organization.*\n> \n> *However, as I mentioned early on, I\u2019m in a great position at Meta as an IC6 with strong performance and significant unvested equity. Because compensation and career trajectory are critical to me, leaving my current role for an L6 position with this compensation package would represent a lateral step financially and a step down in scope.*\n> \n> *To make this transition compelling and compensate for the equity I\u2019d be walking away from, I would need a package that reflects the top of the level band:*\n> * *Base salary: **$255,000***\n> * *Equity: **$1,250,000** over 4 years*\n> * *Sign-on bonus: **$100,000***\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers (or revisit calibration at L7), I would be ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Decision Rule\n* **If they meet your counter or offer L7:** Consider accepting if you want a change in product domain or company culture.\n* **If they refuse to negotiate or make minimal adjustments (e.g., +$10k stock, no sign-on):** **Walk away.** Staying at Meta as an IC6 will yield higher comp stability and clearer progression than entering Google at L6 on an unoptimized package.",
